                                                        <script type="text/javascript">
                                                            //displayArticles({"IssueId":5808,"ProductCode":null,"Year":2026,"Volume":63,"IssueNumber":4,"IssueDirectory":"2026-63-04","FreeFlag":"Y","EndMatterUrl":"/2026-63-04/bull-63-4-print-matter.pdf","FileUrlHost":"https://www.ams.org/journals/bull","HomeUrlHost":"https://pubs.ams.org/bull","DirectFileUrl":"","Articles":[{"ArticleId":93036,"JournalProductCode":"BULL","IssueId":5808,"ManuscriptId":1883,"DocumentType":"ARTICLE","Title":"Finding arithmetic progressions in dense sets of integers","DOI":"10.1090/bull/1883","PII":"S0273-0979-2026-01883-8","Issue":null,"IssueDirectory":"2026-63-04","StartPage":531,"EndPage":557,"PostDate":"2026-08-12T00:00:00","PrimaryMsc":"11B30","SecondaryMsc":null,"MscSchema":"2020","Abstract":"One of the central problems in additive combinatorics is to determine how large a subset of the first $N$ integers can be before it is forced to contain $k$ elements forming an arithmetic progression. Around twenty-five years ago, Gowers proved the first reasonable upper bounds in this problem for progressions of length four and longer. In this work, Gowers initiated the study of \u0026ldquo;higher-order Fourier analysis,\u0026rdquo; which has developed over the past couple of decades into a rich theory with numerous other combinatorial applications. I will report on some very recent progress in higher-order Fourier analysis and how it has led to the first ever quantitative improvement on Gowers\u0026rsquo;s upper bounds when $k\\geq 5$.","EnhancedReferences":"\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Behrend%2C%20F.%20A.\u0022\u003EF. A. Behrend\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003EOn sets of integers which contain no three terms in arithmetical progression\u003C/em\u003E, Proc. Nat. Acad. Sci. U.S.A. \u003Cstrong\u003E32\u003C/strong\u003E (1946), 331\u0026ndash;332.  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=18694\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E18694\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E, DOI \u003Ca href=\u0022https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.32.12.331\u0022\u003E10.1073/pnas.32.12.331\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Bergelson%2C%20Vitaly\u0022\u003EVitaly Bergelson\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Host%2C%20Bernard\u0022\u003EBernard Host\u003C/a\u003E, and \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Kra%2C%20Bryna\u0022\u003EBryna Kra\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003EMultiple recurrence and nilsequences\u003C/em\u003E, Invent. Math. \u003Cstrong\u003E160\u003C/strong\u003E (2005), no.\u0026nbsp;2, 261\u0026ndash;303. With an appendix by Imre Ruzsa.  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2138068\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E2138068\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E, DOI \u003Ca href=\u0022https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-004-0428-6\u0022\u003E10.1007/s00222-004-0428-6\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E T. F. Bloom and O. Sisask, \u003Cem\u003EAn improvement to the Kelley-Meka bounds on three-term arithmetic progressions\u003C/em\u003E, Preprint, arXiv:\u003Ca href=\u0022https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.02353\u0022\u003E2309.02353\u003C/a\u003E, (2023).\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Bloom%2C%20Thomas%20F.\u0022\u003EThomas F. Bloom\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Sisask%2C%20Olof\u0022\u003EOlof Sisask\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003EThe Kelley-Meka bounds for sets free of three-term arithmetic progressions\u003C/em\u003E, Essent. Number Theory \u003Cstrong\u003E2\u003C/strong\u003E (2023), no.\u0026nbsp;1, 15\u0026ndash;44.  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=4681467\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E4681467\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E, DOI \u003Ca href=\u0022https://doi.org/10.2140/ent.2023.2.15\u0022\u003E10.2140/ent.2023.2.15\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Conze%2C%20Jean-Pierre\u0022\u003EJean-Pierre Conze\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Lesigne%2C%20Emmanuel\u0022\u003EEmmanuel Lesigne\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003ESur un th\u0026eacute;or\u0026egrave;me ergodique pour des mesures diagonales\u003C/em\u003E, Probabilit\u0026eacute;s, Publ. Inst. Rech. Math. Rennes, vol.\u0026nbsp;1987, Univ. Rennes I, Rennes, 1988, pp.\u0026nbsp;1\u0026ndash;31 (French).  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=989141\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E989141\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E C. Elsholtz, Z. Hunter, L. Proske, and L. Sauermann, \u003Cem\u003EImproving Behrend\u0026rsquo;s construction: Sets without arithmetic progressions in integers and over finite fields\u003C/em\u003E, Preprint, arXiv:\u003Ca href=\u0022https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12290\u0022\u003E2406.12290\u003C/a\u003E, (2024).\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Erdos%2C%20Paul\u0022\u003EPaul Erd\u0026ouml;s\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Turan%2C%20Paul\u0022\u003EPaul Tur\u0026aacute;n\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003EOn some sequences of integers\u003C/em\u003E, J. London Math. Soc. \u003Cstrong\u003E11\u003C/strong\u003E (1936), no.\u0026nbsp;4, 261\u0026ndash;264.  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1574918\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E1574918\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E, DOI \u003Ca href=\u0022https://doi.org/10.1112/jlms/s1-11.4.261\u0022\u003E10.1112/jlms/s1-11.4.261\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Furstenberg%2C%20Harry\u0022\u003EHarry Furstenberg\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003EErgodic behavior of diagonal measures and a theorem of Szemer\u0026eacute;di on arithmetic progressions\u003C/em\u003E, J. Analyse Math. \u003Cstrong\u003E31\u003C/strong\u003E (1977), 204\u0026ndash;256.  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=498471\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E498471\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E, DOI \u003Ca href=\u0022https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02813304\u0022\u003E10.1007/BF02813304\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Furstenberg%2C%20Hillel\u0022\u003EHillel Furstenberg\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Weiss%2C%20Benjamin\u0022\u003EBenjamin Weiss\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003EA mean ergodic theorem for $(1/N)\\sum ^N_{n=1}f(T^nx)g(T^{n^2}x)$\u003C/em\u003E, Convergence in ergodic theory and probability (Columbus, OH, 1993) Ohio State Univ. Math. Res. Inst. Publ., vol.\u0026nbsp;5, de Gruyter, Berlin, 1996, pp.\u0026nbsp;193\u0026ndash;227.  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1412607\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E1412607\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Gowers%2C%20W.%20T.\u0022\u003EW. T. Gowers\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003EA new proof of Szemer\u0026eacute;di\u0026rsquo;s theorem for arithmetic progressions of length four\u003C/em\u003E, Geom. Funct. Anal. \u003Cstrong\u003E8\u003C/strong\u003E (1998), no.\u0026nbsp;3, 529\u0026ndash;551.  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1631259\u0022\u003