n algebraic geometry\u003C/em\u003E, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 2016. \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=3617981\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E3617981\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E, DOI \u003Ca href=\u0022https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139062046\u0022\u003E10.1017/CBO9781139062046\u003C/a\u003E \u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E William Fulton, \u003Cem\u003EIntersection theory\u003C/em\u003E, 2nd ed., Ergebnisse der Mathematik und ihrer Grenzgebiete. 3. Folge. A Series of Modern Surveys in Mathematics [Results in Mathematics and Related Areas. 3rd Series. A Series of Modern Surveys in Mathematics], vol.\u0026nbsp;2, Springer-Verlag, Berlin, 1998. \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1644323\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E1644323\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E, DOI \u003Ca href=\u0022https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-1700-8\u0022\u003E10.1007/978-1-4612-1700-8\u003C/a\u003E \u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E William Fulton, \u003Cem\u003EYoung tableaux\u003C/em\u003E, London Mathematical Society Student Texts, vol.\u0026nbsp;35, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1997. With applications to representation theory and geometry. \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1464693\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E1464693\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E \u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E Ezra Miller and Bernd Sturmfels, \u003Cem\u003ECombinatorial commutative algebra\u003C/em\u003E, Graduate Texts in Mathematics, vol.\u0026nbsp;227, Springer-Verlag, New York, 2005. \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2110098\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E2110098\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E \u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E Hermann Schubert, \u003Cem\u003EKalk\u00FCl der abz\u00E4hlenden Geometrie\u003C/em\u003E, Springer-Verlag, Berlin-New York, 1979 (German). Reprint of the 1879 original; With an introduction by Steven L. Kleiman. \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=555576\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E555576\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E \u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E Richard P. Stanley, \u003Cem\u003EEnumerative combinatorics. Vol. 2\u003C/em\u003E, Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics, vol.\u0026nbsp;208, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, [2024] \u00A92024. Second edition [of 1676282]; With an appendix by Sergey Fomin. \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=4621625\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E4621625\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E \u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E James Joseph Sylvester, \u003Cem\u003EThe Collected Mathematical Papers of James Joseph Sylvester, Volume 2\u003C/em\u003E, Cambridge University Press, 1908. \u003Ca href=\u0022https://quod.lib.umich.edu/u/umhistmath/AAS8085.0002.001/1\u0022\u003Ehttps://quod.lib.umich.edu/u/umhistmath/AAS8085.0002.001/1\u003C/a\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E","ReviewNumber":1,"Authors":[{"RelationshipType":"AUTHOR","FullName":"David Anderson","FirstName":"David","MiddleName":" ","LastName":"Anderson","Suffix":" ","MasterCustomerId":" ","MRAuthorId":0,"Email":null,"CustomerSequence":0,"Affiliation":null,"AffiliationSequence":0,"FirstEditor":false,"Url":null,"Description":null,"BookReviewId":7684,"CurrentAddress":null},{"RelationshipType":"AUTHOR","FullName":"William Fulton","FirstName":"William","MiddleName":" ","LastName":"Fulton","Suffix":" ","MasterCustomerId":" ","MRAuthorId":0,"Email":null,"CustomerSequence":0,"Affiliation":null,"AffiliationSequence":0,"FirstEditor":false,"Url":null,"Description":null,"BookReviewId":7684,"CurrentAddress":null},{"RelationshipType":"REVIEWER","FullName":"Alexander Yong","FirstName":"Alexander","MiddleName":" ","LastName":"Yong","Suffix":" ","MasterCustomerId":"YNALTA","MRAuthorId":693975,"Email":"ayong@illinois.edu","CustomerSequence":0,"Affiliation":"Department of Mathematics, University of Illinois","AffiliationSequence":0,"FirstEditor":false,"Url":null,"Description":null,"BookReviewId":7684,"CurrentAddress":null}]}],"AMSCopyrightFlag":"Y","CopyrightOwner":"American Mathematical Society","MRNumber":null,"ArticleCitation":"Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. \u003Cstrong\u003E63\u003C/strong\u003E (2026), 685-691","CrossReferences":"","Abstracts":[],"IsAMSCopyright":true,"PostDateFormatted":"August 12, 2026"}], $("#productDetailCurrentVolumeArticleContainer"));
                                                            //displayVolume({"IssueId":5808,"ProductCode":null,"Year":2026,"Volume":63,"IssueNumber":4,"IssueDirectory":"2026-63-04","FreeFlag":"Y","EndMatterUrl":"/2026-63-04/bull-63-4-print-matter.pdf","FileUrlHost":"https://www.ams.org/journals/bull","HomeUrlHost":"https://pubs.ams.org/bull","DirectFileUrl":"","Articles":[{"ArticleId":93036,"JournalProductCode":"BULL","IssueId":5808,"ManuscriptId":1883,"DocumentType":"ARTICLE","Title":"Finding arithmetic progressions in dense sets of integers","DOI":"10.1090/bull/1883","PII":"S0273-0979-2026-01883-8","Issue":null,"IssueDirectory":"2026-63-04","StartPage":531,"EndPage":557,"PostDate":"2026-08-12T00:00:00","PrimaryMsc":"11B30","SecondaryMsc":null,"MscSchema":"2020","Abstract":"One of the central problems in additive combinatorics is to determine how large a subset of the first $N$ integers can be before it is forced to contain $k$ elements forming an arithmetic progression. Around twenty-five years ago, Gowers proved the first reasonable upper bounds in this problem for progressions of length four and longer. In this work, Gowers initiated the study of \u0026ldquo;higher-order Fourier analysis,\u0026rdquo; which has developed over the past couple of decades into a rich theory with numerous other combinatorial applications. I will report on some very recent progress in higher-order Fourier analysis and how it has led to the first ever quantitative improvement on Gowers\u0026rsquo;s upper bounds when $k\\geq 5$.","EnhancedReferences":"\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Behrend%2C%20F.%20A.\u0022\u003EF. A. Behrend\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003EOn sets of integers which contain no three terms in arithmetical progression\u003C/em\u003E, Proc. Nat. Acad. Sci. U.S.A. \u003Cstrong\u003E32\u003C/strong\u003E (1946), 331\u0026ndash;332.  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=18694\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E18694\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E, DOI \u003Ca href=\u0022https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.32.12.331\u0022\u003E10.1073/pnas.32.12.331\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Bergelson%2C%20Vitaly\u0022\u003EVitaly Bergelson\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Host%2C%20Bernard\u0022\u003EBernard Host\u003C/a\u003E, and \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Kra%2C%20Bryna\u0022\u003EBryna Kra\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003EMultiple recurrence and nilsequences\u003C/em\u003E, Invent. Math. \u003Cstrong\u003E160\u003C/strong\u003E (2005), no.\u0026nbsp;2, 261\u0026ndash;303. With an appendix by Imre Ruzsa.  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2138068\u0022\u003EMR \u003Cstrong\u003E2138068\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/a\u003E, DOI \u003Ca href=\u0022https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-004-0428-6\u0022\u003E10.1007/s00222-004-0428-6\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E T. F. Bloom and O. Sisask, \u003Cem\u003EAn improvement to the Kelley-Meka bounds on three-term arithmetic progressions\u003C/em\u003E, Preprint, arXiv:\u003Ca href=\u0022https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.02353\u0022\u003E2309.02353\u003C/a\u003E, (2023).\n\n\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Bloom%2C%20Thomas%20F.\u0022\u003EThomas F. Bloom\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/search/authors.html?authorName=Sisask%2C%20Olof\u0022\u003EOlof Sisask\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cem\u003EThe Kelley-Meka bounds for sets free of three-term arithmetic progressions\u003C/em\u003E, Essent. Number Theory \u003Cstrong\u003E2\u003C/strong\u003E (2023), no.\u0026nbsp;1, 15\u0026ndash;44.  \u003Ca href=\u0022https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathsc